    "html": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n <title>Article Layout Example</title>\n <style>\n .article-container { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 20px; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; }\n .article-header { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px; }\n .headline { font-size: 2em; margin: 0; }\n .byline { color: #555; }\n .article-content { line-height: 1.6; }\n .author-bio { background: #f9f9f9; padding: 10px; margin-top: 30px; font-size: 0.9em; }\n </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n <article class=\"article-container\">\n <header class=\"article-header\">\n <h1 class=\"headline\">The Art of Writing Clean Code</h1>\n <div class=\"byline\">\n <span>By Alex Johnson</span> |\n <time datetime=\"2023-11-05\">November 5, 2023</time>\n </div>\n </header>\n <section class=\"article-content\">\n <p>Writing clean code is essential for maintaining software quality and ensuring ease of collaboration among developers. This article discusses best practices in achieving clean and readable code.</p>\n <p>Firstly, use meaningful names for variables and functions to enhance understanding. Additionally, keep functions small and focused on a single task. Remember, simplicity is key. Avoid complex logic where possible...</p>\n </section>\n <footer class=\"author-bio\">\n <p>Alex Johnson is a software developer with over a decade of experience in building scalable web applications. He enjoys sharing knowledge through his blog and speaking at tech conferences.</p>\n </footer>\n </article>\n</body>\n</html>",
